From 56b413c58c5fd6e9729d59dca1ba6f92def1da54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 19:38:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Compile a nested body from a kept enclosing scope, and check
 it reproduces
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Item 1-1 names one obstacle — the capture layout — and 0104 settled it: derived
from source alone it never misses across 5 157 sites. What is left is the part
the item does not name. A deferred body must be compiled against the enclosing
compiler's state, and that state is twenty-eight fields deep, on the least
exercised path in the engine, where every one of them is a silent miscompile if
wrong. Reproducing it is not a thing to attempt; keeping it is.

FastFunctionScope makes keeping it cheap, and this is the fact the design rests
on: it is not pooled, its Dispose only pops the stack, so a reference to it
retains the whole Parent chain intact after the CreateFunction frame that built
it has returned. LinkedStack.Switch already re-enters an arbitrary scope. So a
retained context is a reference plus the five FastCompiler fields that are not
reachable from the scope — strictness, ScriptInfo, member-initializer state,
parameter-initializer depth — restored on entry and put back in a finally.

CreateFunction is split into a wrapper that decides retention and a Core the
wrapper and the deferred path both call. The wrapper refuses every one of the
fourteen context-carrying parameters rather than trying to save them, and
refuses a body inside a `with`. Refusing is the correct default here: an
unretained function is compiled eagerly, exactly as before.

**Nothing is deferred yet, deliberately.** The eager path still compiles every
body; BROILER_JS_DEFER_TREE decides only whether a context is kept, and the
mechanism compiles the body a SECOND time and compares. That comparison is the
whole point of building it in this order — it is only possible while both trees
exist, and once the eager one stops being produced there is nothing to check the
deferred one against. Nine fixtures cover capture, three-level transitive
capture, per-iteration `let` cells, shadowing, both self-name shapes, strictness
under 'use strict', and that re-entry leaves the compiler state it found.

On the corpus, 4 811 of 5 723 retained functions (84.1%) reproduce
character-for-character after canonicalising four families of compiler-generated
numbering — site indices and three gensym prefixes, which are counters over a
compilation and so differ by construction on a second pass. Mandreel 100.0%,
PdfJS 99.9%, Box2D 99.5%, jQuery 97.0%, Closure 96.6%, Typescript 49.6%. Each
canonicalisation added moved the number (45.5% → 48.9% → 83.2% → 84.1%) without
once exposing a structural difference, which is evidence about the residual but
not proof; settling it needs a structural comparison rather than a textual one.
